## Why This Is Asked

Probes ability to design robust, observable backpressure and safe schema changes in a live stream, balancing throughput and data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and rate limiting
- Idempotent processing and Kafka transactions
- Lag instrumentation, DLQ, chaos testing
- Safe schema evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate) { 
    this.rate = rate; 
    this.tokens = rate; 
    this.last = Date.now(); 
  }

  tryConsume(n=1) { 
    const now = Date.now(); 
    const elapsed = (now - this.last)/1000; 
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.rate, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;

    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Implementation Strategy

**Backpressure Control:**
1. Deploy token bucket rate limiters at producer entry points
2. Monitor consumer lag via Kafka consumer group metrics
3. Implement adaptive rate adjustment based on lag thresholds
4. Use circuit breakers to isolate problematic topics

**Schema Evolution Safety:**
1. Validate schema changes against compatibility rules
2. Deploy dual-writers during schema transitions
3. Maintain version-aware consumers with fallback handling
4. Use canary deployments for schema changes

**Observability & Recovery:**
1. Instrument lag, throughput, and error metrics
2. Set up automated alerts on threshold breaches
3. Implement rollback procedures for failed schema changes
4. Conduct regular chaos testing of backpressure mechanisms

Why This Is Asked
Time skew in multi-region systems is a common but often overlooked root cause of incorrect analytics and deduplication failures. This question tests the ability to define concrete metrics, set safety thresholds, and implement practical containment and rollback strategies without requiring complex redesign.

Key Concepts
- Event-time vs. processing-time semantics
- Clock synchronization (NTP, monotonic clocks)
- Deduplication keys and idempotent processing
- Dead letter queues and reorder buffers
- Safe rollback criteria and validation

Code Example
```javascript
// Idempotent event processor with deduplication
async function processEvent(event) {
  const cacheKey = `event:${event.event_id}`;

  if (await redis.setnx(cacheKey, 'processing')) {
    try {
      await redis.expire(cacheKey, 86400); // 24-hour TTL
      await processInOrder(event);
    } finally {
      await redis.del(cacheKey);
    }
  } else {
    await sendToDLQ(event, 'duplicate');
  }
}

// Monotonic timestamp enforcement
function enforceMonotonic(event, sourceState) {
  if (event.timestamp >= sourceState.lastTimestamp) {
    sourceState.lastTimestamp = event.timestamp;
    return event;
  }
  return null; // Reject out-of-order event
}
```

